# Confirm before git push

Never push to a remote unless the user asked to **check in**, **push**, or **deploy** in the current thread.

## What counts as a push

- `git push` (any remote/branch)
- `backend/scripts/Push-SuiteDeploy.ps1` (`-Api`, `-Ui`, or both)
- Manual subtree pushes (`git push suite-api …`, `git push suite-ui …`)

Local commits, `git status`, `git diff`, `git log` don't need confirmation.

## Required flow (request = confirmation; do not ask again)

1. Commit pending `backend/` and `frontend/` changes per the deploy rules (lint frontend first; fix errors).
2. `git push origin master` when ahead.
3. Run both production deploys so Actions start:
   ```powershell
   .\backend\scripts\Push-SuiteDeploy.ps1 -Api -Ui
   ```
   Skip a side only when the user names just the API or just the UI.
4. `git push origin` alone does not start the deploy workflows — the subtree pushes do.

Do not push at the end of a task when the user did not ask.
